WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.580
Bacteria do not naturally carry a human insulin gene.

2
00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:06.400
How can they make human insulin?

3
00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:12.880
Before engineered microbes, insulin for patients usually came from cattle and pig

4
00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:18.920
pancreases. Then bacteria carrying human instructions began making the medicine.

5
00:00:22.433 --> 00:00:24.953
Start with DNA that codes for insulin.

6
00:00:26.313 --> 00:00:29.413
Nearly all living things use the same genetic code.

7
00:00:30.553 --> 00:00:35.993
But most human genes contain introns, stretches human cells cut out and bacteria

8
00:00:35.993 --> 00:00:42.673
cannot. A bacterium can read intron-free DNA for a human protein when bacterial

9
00:00:42.673 --> 00:00:44.773
control DNA is present.

10
00:00:45.933 --> 00:00:51.053
The gene is the instruction; the control tab helps the bacterium start reading it.

11
00:00:52.067 --> 00:00:55.827
A restriction enzyme recognizes a DNA sequence.

12
00:00:57.127 --> 00:01:01.507
The yellow tool cuts the green gene and a blue plasmid at matching marks.

13
00:01:02.847 --> 00:01:05.247
Many cuts leave single-stranded sticky ends.

14
00:01:06.547 --> 00:01:08.347
Their shapes show which pieces can pair.

15
00:01:09.587 --> 00:01:11.167
The cuts do not add information.

16
00:01:12.487 --> 00:01:14.627
They create pieces that can be rearranged.

17
00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:18.870
The matching ends meet inside the opened plasmid.

18
00:01:20.190 --> 00:01:21.690
DNA ligase seals the gaps.

19
00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:25.530
The blue loop now carries the green insulin gene.

20
00:01:26.810 --> 00:01:31.770
This is recombinant DNA because DNA from different sources has been joined.

21
00:01:32.930 --> 00:01:36.870
The plasmid can copy separately from the bacterium's main chromosome.

22
00:01:38.050 --> 00:01:39.570
Now change one detail.

23
00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:47.690
Predict this. The plasmid carries insulin DNA, but its control tab does not work in

24
00:01:47.690 --> 00:01:50.650
bacteria. Will the cell make insulin?

25
00:01:56.683 --> 00:02:00.343
No. Carrying a gene is not enough.

26
00:02:01.523 --> 00:02:05.443
The cell must recognize its control DNA and read the gene.

27
00:02:06.803 --> 00:02:09.843
Replace the tab with one the bacterium recognizes.

28
00:02:11.223 --> 00:02:14.823
Now the reader moves, and a green insulin-product block appears.

29
00:02:15.683 --> 00:02:19.963
The instruction was present before, but it could not direct a product.

30
00:02:21.117 --> 00:02:23.697
Only some bacteria take up a plasmid.

31
00:02:24.757 --> 00:02:29.817
The plasmid usually carries an antibiotic-resistance gene for a selection step.

32
00:02:31.037 --> 00:02:34.357
On antibiotic, cells without a plasmid fail to grow.

33
00:02:35.737 --> 00:02:38.797
Surviving colonies are enriched for cells carrying one.

34
00:02:39.957 --> 00:02:45.137
The resistance marker travels on the plasmid; insulin itself does not provide

35
00:02:45.137 --> 00:02:46.137
resistance.

36
00:02:46.867 --> 00:02:51.527
As selected bacteria divide, they copy the plasmid and its added gene.

37
00:02:52.807 --> 00:02:55.547
If the gene is switched on, cells make the product.

38
00:02:56.967 --> 00:03:01.407
Scientists then purify and process that product into insulin medicine.

39
00:03:02.767 --> 00:03:08.247
Each daughter can inherit plasmid copies, so, with selection, the colony keeps the

40
00:03:08.247 --> 00:03:09.247
added instruction.

41
00:03:10.217 --> 00:03:13.557
The moving chain is cut, join, carry, select, and read.

42
00:03:14.917 --> 00:03:18.657
Each step supports the next, but none guarantees the final effect.

43
00:03:19.997 --> 00:03:24.997
Engineering changes a cell's DNA; test the effects, judge each use separately.
